Whetstone 1.2, single precision
==============================

Provenance
----------

  netlib               http://www.netlib.org/benchmark/whetstonec
    Rich Painter's 1998 C conversion of the FORTRAN Whetstone,
    (c) 1998 Painter Engineering, Inc. Its licence requires the
    comment block to travel with the source; it is at the top of
    whetstone.c and must stay there.

  kreier/benchmark     whetstone/Arduino/whetstone1.2.ino
    Gareth Halfacree's Arduino port, based on Thomas Kirchner's,
    which is where the double -> float change comes from.

The float version is the one vendored here, and deliberately. This
emulator's FPU is single precision on every target -- the Cortex-M4F and
M7 have no double unit, and rv_hart_fp implements F and not D -- so the
double-precision original would have measured libgcc's soft float rather
than anything this project implements.

Files
-----

  whetstone.c          the benchmark. The eleven modules are as
                       vendored; the four changes outside them are
                       marked WHET_PORT and listed at the top.

  whetstone_portme.c   printf, and the clock. Not part of the
                       measurement: the clock is read twice, and
                       printf runs after it stops.

Building and running
--------------------

  cmake -B build/host -DEMU_PLATFORM=host
  cmake --build build/host --target guest-whetstone
  ./build/host/emu-host --load 0x80000000 build/host/guest/whetstone.bin

  -DWHET_LOOPS=n       work per run. 100 by default: 10 million
                       Whetstone instructions, 10.7 million guest
                       instructions, about 0.26 s on the host
                       interpreter and 0.13 s on the JIT.

  -DWHET_PRINTOUT=ON   upstream's per-module digest, which is the
                       correctness check. Off for timing runs, as
                       upstream's own comment asks.

Reading the result
------------------

Two cautions, both in docs/performance.md at more length.

The rate is a *guest* figure. On the host, guest time advances one tick
per retired instruction, so the two backends report the same rate and
the difference between them is in the host wall clock instead. On the
board the clock is real, and there the same image does measure this
emulator.

The rate depends on WHET_LOOPS, because modules 7 and 11 are the bulk
of the instruction count and both get cheaper per iteration as their
inputs converge. Always quote the LOOP count with the number.

This image is built `-mabi=ilp32f`, overriding the shared guest ABI.
Without it the multilib's libm is soft-float and the benchmark measures
libgcc rather than the FPU -- 86.0 million guest instructions instead
of 10.7, and 116.3 KIPS instead of 937.9.
